#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/id #uuid "bb96e3e7-5fc4-58ed-a666-41e990e1b96d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8285" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a basketball player scores at least $2$ baskets in a series of $3$ free throws if the probability of scoring a basket on each free throw is $0.55.$\n\nII. The probability of getting $35$ even numbers on $60$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nIII. The probability that a fair spinner with three regions, red, green, and blue, lands on green in $5$ out of $12$ spins." :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=3$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =3\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =1.65 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=60$ and $p=\\dfrac12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=12$ and $p=\\dfrac13.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "4c4e12c4-1593-500d-873d-863b4a825e91" :question/math-academy-id "e-8286" :question/problem "In a city, $44\\%$ of the people have blood type A. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $150$ people are picked at random, then $70$ of them will have blood type A. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7123 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7157 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7190 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7517 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7823 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=150$ and $p = 0.44.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.44 \\\\  & =66 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.44) \\\\  & =84 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (150)(0.44) = 66\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (150)(0.44)(1-0.44) = 36.96.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(66, 36.96).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X = 70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X =70) \\approx P(69.5 \\leq Y \\leq 70.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(69.5\\le Y\\le 70.5) & =P(\\frac{69.5-66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{70.5-66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.58\\le Z\\le 0.74) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.74)-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.74)-P(Z\\le 0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(0.74)-Φ(0.58) \\\\  & =0.7704-0.7190 \\\\  & =0.0514\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 70)\\approx 0.051.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0528.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115146 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "9019ee77-a971-598c-b5b6-4ef903fd5029" :question/math-academy-id "e-8287" :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $50$ times. The archer has a $47\\%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye on each shot. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the archer hits the bull's eye more than $29$ times but less than $35$ times. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.6 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9452 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9463 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9474 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.7 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9554 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9564 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9573 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.8 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9641 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9649 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9656 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 3.0 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 3.1 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9990 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9991 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9991 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 3.2 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9993 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9993 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9994 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.47.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.47 \\\\  & =23.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.47) \\\\  & =26.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (50)(0.47) = 23.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (50)(0.47)(1-0.47) = 12.455.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(23.5, 12.455).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(29 <   X <    35).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(29 <   X <    35) = P(30 \\leq X \\leq 34) \\approx P(29.5 \\leq Y \\leq    34.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(29.5\\le Y<34.5) & =P(\\frac{29.5-23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{34.5-23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.70\\le Z\\le 3.12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.12)-P(Z<1.70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.12)-P(Z\\le 1.70) \\\\  & =Φ(3.12)-Φ(1.70) \\\\  & =0.9991-0.9554 \\\\  & =0.0437\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(29 <   X <  35)  \\approx 0.044.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0436.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "998bd535-075d-5cdc-8946-dcd2da85f0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494668} {:answer/id #uuid "af92cf95-3e09-52d6-b3b3-48d72e62b0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494727} {:answer/id #uuid "b4acbfdf-053d-59b8-ac59-8ef6148fe151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494737} {:answer/id #uuid "bfaa7fcf-5764-5700-a4a7-b0c0dec4d62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494769} {:answer/id #uuid "f1713091-6aaf-5c7b-b2ae-d9adc05629c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "998bd535-075d-5cdc-8946-dcd2da85f0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494668} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a0fb1b1-4050-5c41-8c18-a8d2d95f7a21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25eac356-264f-523c-b19b-74743af10bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132260" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of getting $5$ twos on $15$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nII. The probability of finding more than $5$ defective pairs of shoes in a random sample of $50$ if $3%$ of the pairs are defective.\n\nIII. The probability of getting more than $40$ \"yes\" answers on a yes/no survey in a randomly selected sample of $150$ people if the probability that a person answers \"yes\" is $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=15$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=50$ and $p=0.03.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.03 \\\\  & =1.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=150$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =75 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =75 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b511858-4818-5d57-8bd8-1fe8930c3764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.037" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494372} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2bb00f-cdb9-563a-8c96-93286f07eecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494512} {:answer/id #uuid "715cf66d-11dd-57af-bac0-c97174f16e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.046" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494560} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcc2491-00c7-5189-9306-0c2b27964cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494640} {:answer/id #uuid "b203c66c-4318-5382-af46-3a61a328e257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "715cf66d-11dd-57af-bac0-c97174f16e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.046" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494560} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee77bc02-a4f1-5565-b42d-e9a68c8e93d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b518eb4-0e6a-5c41-b563-8246f9c1143e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132269" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(150,0.48),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=67).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ | $\\,0.1762\\,$ | $\\,0.1736\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | $\\,0.2033\\,$ | $\\,0.2005\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ | $\\,0.2297\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=150$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =72 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.48) \\\\  & =78 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(150)(0.48)=72\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(150)(0.48)(1-0.48)=37.44.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(72,37.44).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=67).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=67)≈P(66.5\\le Y\\le 67.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(66.5\\le Y\\le 67.5) & =P(\\frac{66.5-72}{\\sqrt{37.44}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{67.5-72}{\\sqrt{37.44}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.90\\le Z\\le -0.74) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.74)-P(Z<-0.90) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.74)-P(Z\\le -0.90) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.74)-Φ(-0.90) \\\\  & =0.2297-0.1841 \\\\  & =0.046\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=67)≈0.046.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0468.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c6a4e9-74ca-5582-b0af-97d3ec19601a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494482} {:answer/id #uuid "821cd6eb-46cd-5eb8-b1a0-1a9aa204f81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.073" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494601} {:answer/id #uuid "87c0932e-63f4-5bb4-a48c-af20cb5ab96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494617} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf79bda-c742-597d-9104-da468519214c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.082" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494874} {:answer/id #uuid "f90fc399-9a07-50e3-a059-9866e338d3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.079" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "821cd6eb-46cd-5eb8-b1a0-1a9aa204f81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.073" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494601} :answer-field/id #uuid "f507c9e1-30ff-5ca4-a4e4-d03f72191b3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b726722f-b10f-58df-8eed-f61e3c1a5880" :question/math-academy-id "q-132275" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(85,0.56),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=50).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=85$ and $p=0.56.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =85\\cdot 0.56 \\\\  & =47.6 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =85\\cdot (1-0.56) \\\\  & =37.4 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(85)(0.56)=47.6\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(85)(0.56)(1-0.56)=20.94.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(47.6,20.94).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=50).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5) & =P(\\frac{49.5-47.6}{\\sqrt{20.94}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{50.5-47.6}{\\sqrt{20.94}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.42\\le Z\\le 0.63) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.63)-P(Z<0.42) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.63)-P(Z\\le 0.42) \\\\  & =Φ(0.63)-Φ(0.42) \\\\  & =0.7357-0.6628 \\\\  & =0.073\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈0.073.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0758.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fa2daa2-a228-5079-83e2-440c95a5759c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.118" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494412} {:answer/id #uuid "59e161f4-bc56-55a8-80dc-2bc363955b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494499} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4d972d-f509-5c01-8b4a-2ac3a760213f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494504} {:answer/id #uuid "6c60ddf7-7739-58ba-8644-0468de1d7458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.143" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494539} {:answer/id #uuid "fa72f7d7-cce9-54c5-8025-23615a78b581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.139" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59e161f4-bc56-55a8-80dc-2bc363955b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494499} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea74ac30-fe03-5372-8e51-7d585346e95d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfd541af-299c-58d0-8805-828b25adeb36" :question/math-academy-id "q-132294" :question/problem "Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that a soccer player scores $16$ out of $25$ penalties if the player has a $58%$ probability of scoring each penalty. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.58.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.58 \\\\  & =14.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.58) \\\\  & =10.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(25)(0.58)=14.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(25)(0.58)(1-0.58)=6.09.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(14.5,6.09).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=16).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=16)≈P(15.5\\le Y\\le 16.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15.5\\le Y\\le 16.5) & =P(\\frac{15.5-14.5}{\\sqrt{6.09}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{16.5-14.5}{\\sqrt{6.09}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.41\\le Z\\le 0.81) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.81)-P(Z<0.41) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.81)-P(Z\\le 0.41) \\\\  & =Φ(0.81)-Φ(0.41) \\\\  & =0.7910-0.6591 \\\\  & =0.132\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=16)≈0.132.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.1363.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "691a66a6-f8a5-5d42-a44a-c4fd56f19ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.834" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494530} {:answer/id #uuid "7c83485f-fc05-527b-8ac5-652edd3cc5b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.837" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494586} {:answer/id #uuid "83023c61-f5a8-5b7f-a11e-887b7484c4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.830" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494605} {:answer/id #uuid "88f1ea9b-fe91-518b-836c-b738ddcb3eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.805" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494622} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc223ef-5e68-57a9-8abb-0d5420360510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.808" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "691a66a6-f8a5-5d42-a44a-c4fd56f19ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.834" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494530} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe948d3-cf3f-5d7a-a051-dde7f129c617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "865aae44-8837-5a5c-9a79-489c7c05763a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132495" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(240,0.45),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X>100).$ Round your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1685\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1949\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2236\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=240$ and $p=0.45.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =240\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =108 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =240\\cdot (1-0.45) \\\\  & =132 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(240)(0.45)=108\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(240)(0.45)(1-0.45)=59.4.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(108,59.4).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>100).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>100)=P(X\\ge 101)≈P(Y\\ge 100.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 100.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{100.5-108}{\\sqrt{59.4}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge -0.97) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-0.97) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.97) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.97) \\\\  & =1-0.1660 \\\\  & =0.834\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>100)≈0.834.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.8347.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00211791-98ae-5ba1-b2e7-8f6918fccada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.995" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494320} {:answer/id #uuid "594d08e2-0822-590c-8d72-5d55e33b28f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.997" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494498} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2bc3d2-c9db-5dcc-a332-edf3d005e87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.991" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494548} {:answer/id #uuid "cf962d0a-ba4f-5d4a-9cdf-86447e6df013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.993" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494810} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc689f5-84ba-5d2f-8983-18b666da6119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.999" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594d08e2-0822-590c-8d72-5d55e33b28f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.997" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494498} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23856fd-2bc7-50d7-8813-dede2be61e2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fff943eb-16aa-5bd0-bfca-c95efa66ad92" :question/math-academy-id "q-132501" :question/problem "In a biased coin, $60%$ of the flips land on heads. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the coin lands on heads at least $21$ times out of $50$ flips. Give your final answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0017\\,$ | $\\,0.0016\\,$ | $\\,0.0016\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0023\\,$ | $\\,0.0023\\,$ | $\\,0.0022\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0032\\,$ | $\\,0.0031\\,$ | $\\,0.0030\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(50)(0.6)=30\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(50)(0.6)(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(30,12).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 21).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 21)≈P(Y>20.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>20.5) & =1-P(Y\\le 20.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{20.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le -2.74) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.74) \\\\  & =1-0.0031 \\\\  & =0.9969\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 21)≈0.997.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.9966.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35350a1f-7895-51fc-8e71-c814db467aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.583" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494424} {:answer/id #uuid "54feab7b-9521-5920-abd6-97ca8ad85f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494490} {:answer/id #uuid "65d9e86c-9f69-5f93-b937-a18b030341ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.626" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494523} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bdf78a-e3f2-5b0b-8bcf-024af2813423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.663" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494834} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5d40eb-d6c1-59c7-b0ed-ebf1039c7633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.579" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54feab7b-9521-5920-abd6-97ca8ad85f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494490} :answer-field/id #uuid "00cb2415-3f5b-552e-bb6f-9f456d752a78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "212cd403-0726-5b1b-ae69-c8952ab761bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132503" :question/problem "In a math test, a student has a $55%$ probability of correctly answering each question. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that a student correctly solves more than $10$ questions of a $20$-question test. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3446\\,$ | $\\,0.3409\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3821\\,$ | $\\,0.3783\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=20$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =11 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20\\cdot (1-0.55) \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(20)(0.55)=11\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(20)(0.55)(1-0.55)=4.95.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(11,4.95).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>10).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>10)=P(X\\ge 11)≈P(Y\\ge 10.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 10.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{10.5-11}{\\sqrt{4.95}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge -0.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-0.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.22) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.22) \\\\  & =1-0.4129 \\\\  & =0.5871\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>10)≈0.587.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.5914.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c65a9f-2e2e-52e5-b194-f2ed8af16558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494331} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7a3324-d339-595f-b7ea-55311864bfea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494447} {:answer/id #uuid "5af482ec-476b-5c66-8fed-f5647158a2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494503} {:answer/id #uuid "a6eb8422-bc12-5384-8435-5c69b3ff70cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494703} {:answer/id #uuid "b72955e4-be8c-54ec-be7d-7e25e01e936f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b72955e4-be8c-54ec-be7d-7e25e01e936f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494744} :answer-field/id #uuid "542b7abb-095b-5961-a340-01b18bc5861a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bda691e-a546-5a71-b17f-20d3338df2d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95695" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of a student getting $7$ correct answers on a quiz with $10$ questions if each question has $4$ options and the student answers all questions at random.\n\nII. The probability of getting $35$ ones on $60$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nIII. The probability of having $3$ girls in a family of $7$ children if the probability that a randomly selected child is a girl is $0.48.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=10$ and $p=0.25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=60$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=7$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =7\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =3.36 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1f13b1a-8de4-5337-b09e-91782377760d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114884} {:answer/id #uuid "ea365193-b90a-58ac-a1f2-322193762b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114885} {:answer/id #uuid "1d648d3c-b79a-5e25-8044-84600a900a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114886} {:answer/id #uuid "51c6f36a-e07d-5179-a0e6-ee9c73b96778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114887} {:answer/id #uuid "7328fd99-74d5-5b0e-af0d-58f247b1149e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51c6f36a-e07d-5179-a0e6-ee9c73b96778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114887} :answer-field/id #uuid "c12a1c0d-d919-5d9c-ba5d-231ad9aadde1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0991d4ae-bc93-583a-a172-d0ca1666f01a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95696" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of getting $12$ defective bolts in a box containing $20$ bolts if the probability that an individual bolt is defective is $0.2.$\n\nII. The probability that a soccer player scores $20$ of $30$ penalties if the probability of scoring each penalty is $0.55.$\n\nIII. The probability of getting $9$ fives on $28$ rolls of a $6$-sided die." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=20$ and $p=0.2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=30$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =30\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =16.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30\\cdot (1-0.55) \\\\  & =13.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=28$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =28\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc12929-212a-53ad-b5cf-1776af111aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494442} {:answer/id #uuid "5b94ddb7-793b-5a9b-9679-c0ce9e61e2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494505} {:answer/id #uuid "5f137ed0-57e9-5fca-a4b2-ff2aa08efa47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494516} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfbde10-be8f-5681-9a28-d3fccf5c0410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494585} {:answer/id #uuid "adaf26d5-45a4-5ebe-9d67-93154896144e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bc12929-212a-53ad-b5cf-1776af111aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494442} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3237761-34f1-5ac9-9936-425deb983312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "836c76f0-231f-583b-b08e-df25ef466c6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95699" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a fair spinner with five regions, red, green, yellow, orange, and blue, lands on yellow in $8$ out of $15$ spins.\n\nII. The probability that a soccer player misses a penalty if the probability of scoring the penalty is $0.48.$\n\nIII. The probability that an archer hits the bull's eye more than $10$ times out of $25$ attempts if the probability of hitting the bull's eye on a single shot is $0.56.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=15$ and $p=\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=1$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =1\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =0.48 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.56.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.56 \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.56) \\\\  & =11 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c9a7ad-3564-5e24-a66a-604cb6cb332b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494434} {:answer/id #uuid "48772f91-3e50-57b1-b7f1-d6c0568250c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494465} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3131fb-d59c-57b2-930f-2bcd721dbc80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494755} {:answer/id #uuid "bba92cf1-0f0e-567f-9bd8-3938ce1c0d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494758} {:answer/id #uuid "d502be3d-c7bc-5b6f-a8e9-be63c39083a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48772f91-3e50-57b1-b7f1-d6c0568250c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494465} :answer-field/id #uuid "40071c65-69af-5622-b441-b1075a6ee30d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "149f319f-bbde-53de-865d-1514ecb7ea83" :question/math-academy-id "q-95715" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of finding more than $120$ people with blood type O+ in a randomly selected sample of $200$ if $53%$ of the population has blood type O+.\n\nII. The probability of finding less than $4$ defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine if $2%$ of the items made are defective.\n\nIII. The probability that a biased coin lands on heads $9$ times out of $15$ coin flips if the probability of a single flip landing on heads is $0.25.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.53.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.53 \\\\  & =106 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.53) \\\\  & =94 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.02.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=15$ and $p=0.25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =3.75 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87044dd2-9dec-5037-baed-466b750f914c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.014" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494616} {:answer/id #uuid "99750630-e4cc-503e-9185-a49d3e7b138b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494667} {:answer/id #uuid "a666113c-0a9a-5270-8a84-1103dd3a6c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494699} {:answer/id #uuid "aa306e23-7ca1-53c8-939a-84d254417478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494711} {:answer/id #uuid "aad33263-b593-5f72-9670-762a2eec16d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.141" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99750630-e4cc-503e-9185-a49d3e7b138b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494667} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e37936-2de1-5d34-8dd5-3c2259d1d17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed942805-cf1d-5ddc-afc6-5eb5833ba5cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95743" :question/problem "In an airport, $58%$ of the flights arrive early. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $60$ flights are picked at random, then $40$ of them will arrive early. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=60$ and $p=0.58.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot 0.58 \\\\  & =34.8 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-0.58) \\\\  & =25.2 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(60)(0.58)=34.8\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(60)(0.58)(1-0.58)=14.62.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(34.8,14.62).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=40).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=40)≈P(39.5\\le Y\\le 40.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(39.5\\le Y\\le 40.5) & =P(\\frac{39.5-34.8}{\\sqrt{14.62}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{40.5-34.8}{\\sqrt{14.62}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.23\\le Z\\le 1.49) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.49)-P(Z<1.23) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.49)-P(Z\\le 1.23) \\\\  & =Φ(1.49)-Φ(1.23) \\\\  & =0.9319-0.8907 \\\\  & =0.0412\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=40)≈0.041.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0415.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec81fad-4d2f-5c95-95d4-c12a0665f84c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.788" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494408} {:answer/id #uuid "54bba7fc-c3f0-5077-b2c5-9c6593c05ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.787" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494487} {:answer/id #uuid "86dc79e8-7c69-5747-a450-f6b2d99e6665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.778" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494615} {:answer/id #uuid "b791b483-da31-5616-ac75-ddcf6a6d3b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.781" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494747} {:answer/id #uuid "e25c814a-f71c-51da-8fd0-0cf9e3022b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.718" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec81fad-4d2f-5c95-95d4-c12a0665f84c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.788" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494408} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb664b9-db89-50b9-9205-2e13e64ccf73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "036e3017-f74f-5cab-a417-486991609067" :question/math-academy-id "q-95749" :question/problem "On average, $53%$ of the emails Emily receives are spam. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that she has $70$ or more spam emails in her inbox if she has $140$ emails in her inbox. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=140$ and $p=0.53.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =140\\cdot 0.53 \\\\  & =74.2 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =140\\cdot (1-0.53) \\\\  & =65.8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(140)(0.53)=74.2\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(140)(0.53)(1-0.53)=34.874.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(74.2,34.874).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 70)≈P(Y\\ge 69.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 69.5) & =1-P(Y<69.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<\\frac{69.5-74.2}{\\sqrt{34.874}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z<-0.80) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.80) \\\\  & =1-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 70)≈0.788.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.7869.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "783013b8-2e23-5c9c-96aa-b7e3c5fa0e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.244" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494578} {:answer/id #uuid "80665550-bbd8-558f-b16b-ff1eb939773c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494598} {:answer/id #uuid "82c2a6d1-e3d7-55e9-b78e-a5e51b93d945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.402" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494604} {:answer/id #uuid "a4852c75-4ac7-5fb8-b281-76d948d26df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494693} {:answer/id #uuid "c1275fca-a908-56a0-a111-7ff676237282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80665550-bbd8-558f-b16b-ff1eb939773c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494598} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cf5633b-0c34-58f1-9af7-e4105434c5a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d886c0f0-1fbe-59f2-a61e-06cc1aef64d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95775" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(120,0.6),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=80).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9032\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9049\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9066\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9192\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9207\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9222\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9332\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9345\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9357\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9147\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9162\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9177\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9292\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9306\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9319\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9418\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9429\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9441\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=120$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =120\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =72 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =12\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =48 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(120)(0.6)=72\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(120)(0.6)(1-0.6)=28.8.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(72,28.8).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=80).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=80)≈P(79.5\\le Y\\le 80.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(79.5\\le Y\\le 80.5) & =P(\\frac{79.5-72}{\\sqrt{28.8}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{80.5-72}{\\sqrt{28.8}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.40\\le Z\\le 1.58) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.58)-P(Z<1.40) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.58)-P(Z\\le 1.40) \\\\  & =Φ(1.58)-Φ(1.40) \\\\  & =0.9429-0.9192 \\\\  & =0.0237\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=80)≈0.024.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0245.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386ffdee-0a9c-5c48-9620-f36a06ff13fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.473" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494435} {:answer/id #uuid "4c710fdf-cb10-5d92-8c25-86c325e7bc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.487" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494473} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7ddccf-b8ca-503f-bee5-847a976cfb15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.503" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494638} {:answer/id #uuid "bb429d2f-5684-55ac-95e1-cd71db6ea25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.511" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494757} {:answer/id #uuid "df7f9485-4dc8-5660-afc2-33f733c110d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.492" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df7f9485-4dc8-5660-afc2-33f733c110d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.492" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494846} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0750819-f5ed-5c61-8873-54bb6b64a700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01c1e9f3-92c7-5436-aa3f-7f0248f68a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-95816" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(70,0.52),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X\\ge 37).$ Round your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4602\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.5040\\,$ | $\\,0.5080\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=70$ and $p=0.52.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =70\\cdot 0.52 \\\\  & =36.4 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =70\\cdot (1-0.52) \\\\  & =33.6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(70)(0.52)=36.4\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(70)(0.52)(1-0.52)=17.472.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(36.4,17.472).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 37).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 37)≈P(Y\\ge 36.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 36.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{36.5-36.4}{\\sqrt{17.472}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge 0.02) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.02) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.02) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.02) \\\\  & =1-0.5080 \\\\  & =0.4920\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 37)≈0.492.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.4905.$"}]}
